{
  "standing": "Normative and closed. A policy atom may name a relation from exactly two places: the thirteen NATIVE fact relations closed by foundation/relation-payload-schemas.v2.json#/x-opensip-relation-registry, or the imported-evidence relations below. An atom naming anything else refuses at policy admission; there is no open relation namespace and no default.",
  "why": "Atoms range over imported-evidence subjects as well as native facts, and the same per-relation ladder law must decide their minResolution. Without a ladder for these relations the atom's rung is undecidable, which is the other half of the withdrawn abstract-tier vocabulary. These relations are deliberately NOT added to the native fact registry: they mint no fact2, they carry no sourceUniverse/targetUniverse, and that registry stays closed at thirteen.",
  "ladderRule": "Same law as native relations: the ladder is that relation's own total order, weakest-first, and minResolution must be a member of it. Nothing orders a rung of one relation against a rung of another.",
  "rungVocabulary": "No new rung token is introduced. `observed` is already the registered rung of the native `unresolved-edge` relation, and an import carries exactly one strength of claim: the observation was made. Window, population, observability and completeness are carried by the payload and the closed-world machinery, never by the rung.",
  "evidenceDeclarationRule": "An atom naming one of these relations MUST carry `evidence` equal to that relation's evidenceKind, and its rule must declare a matching evidenceUse. An atom naming a native fact relation MUST NOT carry `evidence`. This keeps the two planes separable at admission rather than by convention."
}
